/-!
# Γ₀(3) is generated by T, U and -1

Theorem statement: `CongruenceSubgroup.closure_T_U_neg_one_eq_Gamma0_three`
(`ee2e87fa-6ddf-5fb1-b8a6-43ad24a595b2`), by Claude, 2026-09-05.

Proof: submission `d00a3882-a8c7-5dbc-8397-bc8df7d69c69`, by Claude, 2026-09-05 (ACCEPTED); locally
adapted.

Let $U$ be an element of $\mathrm{SL}_2(\mathbb{Z})$ whose underlying integer $2\times 2$ matrix is
$\begin{pmatrix} 1 & 0 \\ -3 & 1\end{pmatrix}$. The assertion is an equality of subgroups of
$\mathrm{SL}_2(\mathbb{Z})$: the subgroup generated by the three-element set consisting of
`ModularGroup.T`, that is the translation matrix $\begin{pmatrix} 1 & 1 \\ 0 & 1\end{pmatrix}$, of
$U$, and of the central element $-1$, coincides with the congruence subgroup
`CongruenceSubgroup.Gamma0 3`, namely the subgroup of those elements of $\mathrm{SL}_2(\mathbb{Z})$
whose lower-left entry is congruent to $0$ modulo $3$. Since the equality is of subgroups, both
inclusions are contained in it: each of $T$, $U$ and $-1$ has lower-left entry divisible by $3$, and
conversely every matrix in $\Gamma_0(3)$ is a word in these three elements and their inverses.

This is the classical determination, going back to Rademacher, of generators for $\Gamma_0(3)$: the
stabiliser $T$ of the cusp $\infty$, the stabiliser $U$ of the cusp $0$ (a conjugate of $T^{-1}$ by
the Fricke involution $W_3 = \begin{pmatrix} 0 & -1 \\ 3 & 0\end{pmatrix}$), together with the
centre. It is used in
[`ModularForm.exists_weight_one_gamma1_three_slash_fricke_eq_smul`]
(thm.html#ModularForm.exists_weight_one_gamma1_three_slash_fricke_eq_smul),
where the behaviour of a weight one form of level $3$ under the Fricke involution is pinned down by
checking finitely many generators.

